###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Training one model takes 35.4 hours on a single A100 after the single-GPU
# optimisations and 1.5 hours with 32 GPUs (title claim); strong and weak
# scaling up to 32 GPUs are reported as efficiencies. Recorded as text in
# trainingHardware; the per-step MD times of Table II in inferenceHardware.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - FastCHGNet is encoded as an implementation of the registry method entity:CHGNet. Its 'F/S head' version predicts forces and stresses with separate heads rather than as derivatives of the energy, which is a different functional form in a strict sense; if that distinction matters for queries it needs its own registry method.
# - Magnetic-moment MAEs (37 / 35 / 36 m-mu_B) have no MetricProperty. The R^2 values and the learning-rate-tuning experiment at batch size 2048 (15 meV/atom, 72 meV/A, 0.476 GPa) are not encoded.
# - Which version the 35.4 h and 1.5 h figures and the Table II timings refer to is not stated; they are attached to the 'F/S head' model, the final FastCHGNet.
# - The 'radial and angular basis number is set to 31' is encoded as numRadialBasis = numAngularBasis = 31; the 64-dimensional atom/bond/angle embeddings as numFeatures 64.
# - The manifest classes this paper as 'Training/reliability'; it is a software-performance paper.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: crossref).
